WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:02.249
No one did more to mainstream

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.109
libertarian ideas about peace, love

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:05.909
and understanding over the past half

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:07.469
century than P.J.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.299
O'Rourke, who has died at

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.279
the age of 74.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:12.989
And like Frank Sinatra, Elvis

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:15.179
Presley and Sid Vicious, P.J.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.039
did it his way by taking

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.959
a blowtorch to the sacred cows

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.089
of both the left and right.

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:22.649
"The Democrats are the party that

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:23.909
says government will make you

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:26.099
smarter, taller, richer and remove

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.839
the crabgrass on your lawn," he

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:28.649
warned.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:30.119
"The Republicans are the party that

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:31.649
says government doesn't work and

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.839
then they get elected and prove it."

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.759
Writing in popular outlets such as

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.499
National Lampoon, Rolling Stone and

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.269
The Atlantic, and appearing on shows

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:41.309
like NPR's Wait, Wait Don't Tell

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.409
Me, O'Rourke distilled the insights

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.209
of Milton Friedman, Ayn Rand and

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.219
Friedrich Hayek with far more oomph

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:48.869
than they could ever manage.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:50.129
"Giving money and power to the

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:51.779
government is like giving whiskey

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:53.849
and car keys to teenage boys,"

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:54.839
wrote O'Rourke.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.129
"When buying and selling are

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.109
controlled by legislation, the first

00:00:58.110 --> 00:00:59.699
things to be bought and sold are

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.799
legislators." "Libertarianism

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.899
isn't political," he insisted.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.819
"It's anti-political, really.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.679
It wants to take things out of the

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.179
political arena." Like his

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:11.069
journalistic inspirations, Tom

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.029
Wolfe and Hunter S.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.279
Thompson, O'Rourke was no armchair

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.109
curmudgeon or ivory-tower

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:17.339
philosopher king.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:19.529
At his very best, in books like

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:21.059
All the Trouble in the World and

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:22.829
Holidays in Hell, he engaged the

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:24.689
world directly and often at great

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.609
personal risk, traveling to war

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.379
zones and disaster areas in more

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.629
than 40 countries, including urban

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:32.369
and rural hellholes in the United

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:32.999
States.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.279
I have always

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.469
belonged to the pessimistic

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.959
wing of the libertarian

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:41.909
attitude.

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:43.919
This is probably because I spent 20

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.659
years as a foreign correspondent,

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:47.279
largely covering wars and

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.319
insurrections and social

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.269
upheavals and disturbances

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:52.859
of all sorts of ugly cons.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.779
We have a rational side,

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:55.889
thank God.

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:58.019
But it isn't the only side

00:01:58.020 --> 00:01:59.879
in our multifaceted

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:01.769
and sometimes pretty ugly little

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:03.419
personalities.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:04.739
Even as he despaired over a

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:06.539
presidential contest between Joe

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:08.698
Biden and Donald Trump, he was never

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:10.109
dissuaded from his faith in

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:12.239
individuals yearning to be free,

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:13.919
whether they live in Chicago or

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.539
China. "At the core of

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.459
libertarianism, as an attitude

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:18.779
and as a way of thinking about

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:20.609
politics," he said, "is

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:23.339
the idea that people are assets."

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.379
His elegiac 2014

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:27.269
book, The Baby Boom: How It Got That

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:28.649
Way and It Wasn't My Fault and I'll

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:30.419
Never Do It Again, writes his

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:31.349
epitaph.

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:32.969
Far from being either a screed

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:34.949
against his own generation, which

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:36.479
is what he expected it to be when he

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.429
started writing, or an attack

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:40.259
on millennials, it was instead a

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.119
funny and thoughtful meditation

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:44.009
on how we've arrived at a kinder,

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:46.079
gentler country that somehow manages

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.909
to prize individualism and

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.039
community, innovation and

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:51.059
tradition.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:52.379
When asked whether the fights

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:53.819
between the greatest generation

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:55.499
boomers and millennials had left the

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.479
country weirder and better off,

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.429
he told Reason, "I think so."

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:00.449
Certainly more tolerant.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.519
In fact, tolerance isn't even a

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.169
good word anymore because tolerance

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:05.939
means, Well, I'll put up with you if

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:06.940
I have to, you know,

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:10.649
is more enthusiastic

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:12.299
about people's differences, you

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.189
know, applauding

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:16.469
them, embracing them, as it were.

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:17.999
And that's, that's good.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.039
If that's true, it's in no small

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:21.509
part due to the contribution

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:23.759
O'Rourke offered up, first

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:25.649
by making us laugh, then

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:28.289
by making us think, and finally

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:30.149
by making us want to go out into

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:32.219
the world he traveled with such

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:33.220
passion.

